{{! The set's formula, and whose notation it is.

    One partial rather than markup in each branch that wants a formula, because
    the two lines are one statement: most set formulas are quoted from an outside
    compilation rather than authored here, and a reader who meets the string first
    has already read it as the platform's. Rendering the notation without the
    credit is the failure worth making impossible, so the credit is not a sibling
    element a later edit can drop but the thing this partial leads with.

    Where a set has no formula, the section says so rather than showing an empty
    code block: the launch mechanics can be settled while the bracket-grammar form
    is not yet authored, and those are different states.

    Whether a page that teaches its notation in prose should also show the raw
    string is a content question this partial does not answer. It answers only
    what happens once a page decides to show one. }}
<section class="set-detail-section" aria-label="Formula">
  <h2>Formula</h2>
  {{#if content.formula}}
    {{#if content.formulaSource}}
    <p class="set-detail-component-note">Source: {{content.formulaSource}}</p>
    <code class="set-detail-formula">{{content.formula}}</code>
    {{else}}
    {{! Unreachable while the derivation refuses a formula whose citation names no
        source. Kept as the safe branch anyway: an unattributed formula is the one
        outcome this page must never produce, so the missing credit is reported
        rather than the notation shown bare. }}
    <p class="set-detail-component-note">This set&rsquo;s formula is withheld: its source is not recorded, and the notation is not the platform&rsquo;s to present unattributed.</p>
    {{/if}}
  {{else}}
  <p class="set-detail-component-note">Canonical JOB notation is pending: the launch mechanics are defined, but the bracket-grammar form is not yet authored.</p>
  {{/if}}
</section>
